    ":question/id": "db3aefbf-446d-55a9-a2e6-d344640795c1",
    ":question/problem": "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form $R_{i}:=R_{i}+kR_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?",
    ":question/worked-solution": "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries above $a_{32}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{R_{1}:=R_{1}-2R_{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{R_{2}:=R_{2}-2R_{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=B\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$R_{1}:=R_{1}-2R_{3}$ and $R_{2}:=R_{2}-2R_{3}$.",
